Standardized Statistic Review

Another way to test for strength of evidence in favor of H_{A}
Obtain the standard deviation (SD)
Calculate the standardized statistic (z) by:

z=\frac{Observed\: Statistic - Hypothesized\: Value}{Standard\: Deviation\: under\: the\: null}

2SD (95%) Confidence Interval for Proportions
CI=\hat{p}±2*\sqrt{\frac{\hat{p}*(1-\hat{p})}{n}}
=(Lower\:Bound,Upper\:Bound)

2SD (95%) Confidence Interval for Means
CI=\bar{x}±2*\frac{s}{\sqrt{n}}
=(Lower\:Bound,Upper\:Bound)

Recall…

Rejecting the null hypothesis means we have statistical significance in favor of H_{A}
Failing to reject the null hypothesis means we do not have statistical significance in favor of H_{A}

Theory Based Approach (Quantitative Variable)

With numerical data, we use “t” instead of “z” to describe our standardized statistic

Standardized\: Statistic\:= t=\frac{\bar{x}-\mu}{SE}=\frac{\bar{x}-\mu}{\frac{s}{\sqrt{n}}}

Formulas
Standard error for means:
SE=\frac{s}{\sqrt{n}}
2SD (95%) Confidence Interval for Means:
CI=\bar{x}±2*\frac{s}{\sqrt{n}}

Why does it make sense that we use \bar{x} now?

Using Theory for Standardized Values
Replace S.D. with theory-based calculation
z=\frac{Observed\: Statistic - Hypothesized\: Value}{Standard\: Deviation\: under\: the\: null}
=\frac{\hat{p}-\pi}{\sqrt{\frac{\pi(1-\pi)}{n}}}

Observed statistic?

In a normal scenario, our observed statistic would be:

\hat{p}=\frac{74}{1641}
AKA:
\hat{p}=\frac{Total\:shifts\:a\:patient\:died}{Total\:sample\:size}
But, this isn’t super helpful!

3S Strategy: Statistic
First, we need a statistic. What is it that we’re interested in?

Parameter: The difference in long run proportions between two groups

\pi_{Group\:1} - \pi_{Group\:2}




Observed Statistic: The difference in conditional proportions

\hat{p}_{Group\:1} - \hat{p}_{Group\:2}

Standardized Statistic (SS)
General form:
SS=\frac{Observed\: Statistic - Hypothesized\: Value}{Standard\: Deviation\: under\: the\: null}
=\frac{(\hat{p}_1-\hat{p}_2)-0}{SD\: from\: Applet}

Standardized Statistic for Two Groups
General standardized statistic (SS) formula:
SS=\frac{Observed\: Statistic - Hypothesized\: Value}{Standard\: Error\: under\: the\: null}
When comparing two groups:
z=\frac{(\hat{p}_{1} - \hat{p}_{2})-0}{\sqrt{\hat{p}(1-\hat{p})(\frac{1}{n_1}+\frac{1}{n_2})}}

Calculating the Standardized Statistic
z=\frac{\hat{p}_{D} - \hat{p}_{L}}{\sqrt{\hat{p}(1-\hat{p})(\frac{1}{n_D}+\frac{1}{n_L})}}
=\frac{0.10 - 0.39}{\sqrt{0.29(1-0.29)(\frac{1}{172}+\frac{1}{307})}}
=-6.71

Calculating the Confidence Interval
CI=(\hat{p}_D-\hat{p}_L)±2*\sqrt{\frac{\hat{p}_D(1-\hat{p}_D)}{n_D}+\frac{\hat{p}_L(1-\hat{p}_L)}{n_L}}
=(0.10-0.39)±2*\sqrt{\frac{0.10*0.90}{172}+\frac{0.39*0.61}{307}}
=(-0.362,-0.218)
